From: buntOS contributors
Subject: [PATCH] drm/panel: novatek-nt36532: add the Lenovo TB375FC panels

The Lenovo Xiaoxin Pad Pro 12.7 (2025, TB375FC) and the Lenovo Idea Tab Pro
(TB373FU) ship with one of three NT36532 panels: BOE, Tianma, or a matte
Tianma. All have a 2944x1840 active area of 273.6x171 mm, driven at 144,
120, 60 or 30 Hz over two DSI links of four lanes with DSC 1.1, two 736x20
slices per link and 8 bits per pixel.

The init sequences, timings and DSC parameters come from the vendor's panel
drivers. They match the panel modules of the stock firmware and the tables
of its bootloader byte for byte. The plain and the matte Tianma panel have
the same sequence, so the driver only tells BOE from Tianma, with the strap
the vendor's touchscreen driver reads as well. The vendor's bootloader
finds the BOE panel by that strap and a second one, then asks the panel for
its ID to tell the Tianma panels apart.

What differs from the CSOT panel:

- The tables behind the refresh rates. Registers 0xb2 and 0xb3 select them,
  so the panel has more than one mode and the init sequence looks up the
  current one in the connector state, as the NT35950 driver does. 120, 60
  and 30 Hz only differ in the vertical front porch. The vendor gives the
  horizontal porches per link. The modes hold the sum of both, as the DSI
  hosts of bonded links split the timings of the mode between them.

- The DSC parameters. The rate control is the DSC 1.1 table from before the
  SCR with a first line offset of 13, as the panels expect the values that
  follow from it: the BOE init sequence even writes them to the panel. The
  host cannot derive that from the version alone, so the panel fills in the
  whole configuration, including the size of the picture of each link.

- The bias supplies. The vendor brings up the positive one, then the
  negative one 2 ms later, and turns them off in the opposite order.

- The backlight. The vendor sets the 12-bit brightness with a DCS command
  to the panel. It also programs two LED drivers on I2C, and writes the
  voltages of the bias chip, neither of which has a driver here yet.

- The command link. The panel node sits under the first DSI host, which
  sends the commands of the vendor's kernel to both links in sync.

The DSI host for this needs dual-DSI support and has to take the DSC
configuration as it comes, so this has not run on the tablet yet.
---
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/Kconfig                 |   5 +++--
 drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c | 530 ++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 2 files changed, 522 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/Kconfig b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/Kconfig
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/Kconfig
@@ -633,8 +633,9 @@
 	help
 	  Say Y here if you want to enable support for the panels built
 	  around the Novatek NT36532 display controller, such as some
-	  CSOT panels used in OnePlus Pad 2 tablets. These panels are
-	  typically dual-DSI and may use DSC (Display Stream Compression).
+	  CSOT panels used in OnePlus Pad 2 tablets and the BOE and Tianma
+	  panels of the Lenovo TB375FC. These panels are typically dual-DSI
+	  and may use DSC (Display Stream Compression).
 
 config DRM_PANEL_NOVATEK_NT36672A
 	tristate "Novatek NT36672A DSI panel"
diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-novatek-nt36532.c
@@ -4,8 +4,12 @@
